Moth uses one `loop` keyword for conditional loops, collection iteration
and numeric ranges.

A conditional loop evaluates a `Bool` condition before each iteration and runs
its body while that condition remains `true`.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    count ~= 0

    loop count < 3:
        io.line([: [count]])
        ~count += 1
    ;
]

### Conditional loop contract

- The form is `loop condition: ... ;`.
- The condition must resolve to `Bool`.
- The condition is checked before the first iteration, so the body may run zero times.
- It is checked again after each completed iteration.
- Conditional loops do not declare item or index bindings.
- There is no separate `while` keyword.
- `break` exits the nearest active loop.
- `continue` skips the rest of the current body and starts the next condition check.

### Divergence proof

A conditional loop whose condition has normalised to compile-time `true` and
which has no reachable `break` targeting that loop diverges. Falling through
the body or executing `continue` starts another iteration. A reachable `break`
targeting the loop makes the post-loop continuation reachable. A nested loop
consumes its own `break` and `continue`. A nested-loop `break` does not make
an outer true loop escapable. Return and divergence pass outward.

Collection and range loops stay conservatively non-divergent because their
iteration count is finite or may be zero.

This proof uses the ordinary folded Bool authority. It does not inspect a
runtime condition to decide that the loop stays true forever.

### Header classification

A top-level `to` marker makes the header a range loop.

Without a range marker, a top-level `by` step or an explicit `|...|` suffix marks
a collection loop. Without bindings or a step, a collection-typed header is still
a collection loop. Every other header must type-check as a `Bool` condition. A
`by` step on a `Bool` condition is rejected instead of being treated as a
collection loop.

### Scope

The body is a child scope. Names declared inside it are not visible after the
loop.
